  "boards": {
    "public": [
      {
        "round": 1,
        "agent": "driver_1",
        "kind": "announcement",
        "text": "plans to drive in zone 0 this wave. Note: First wave planning \u2014 general thoughts"
      },
      {
        "round": 3,
        "agent": "driver_1",
        "kind": "message",
        "text": "I'm still wrestling with the pricing formula ambiguity, and I want to share my best reading so we can lock it down before committing to allocations.\n\nMy best interpretation of the formula:\n\n**price = 10 + 5 \u00d7 max(0, riders_k \u2212 capacity_k), capped at 80**\n\nWhere \"capacity_k\" is the actual number of drivers assigned to zone k (interpretation b). Under this reading, we can control prices by choosing how many drivers go where.\n\nMy alternative reading:\n\n**price = 10 + 5 \u00d7 max(0, riders_k \u2212 4), capped at 80**  (where 4 is the max per-zone capacity)\n\nUnder this reading, the price is essentially fixed except that zones with more riders will cost more. Zone 0 (8 riders) would be the priciest at 30, others at 10.\n\nBefore I commit, I want to know: which reading does the team think is correct? And if we're going with interpretation (b), what allocation would you suggest, and how does it play out on profit?\n\nActually, I realize I haven't really played this yet. Let me start fresh with a clear plan in mind once we nail down the formula."
      }
    ],
    "private": [
      {
        "round": 1,
        "agent": "driver_1",
        "kind": "message",
        "text": "Team planning thoughts for wave 1.\n\nKey facts:\n- 4 drivers, 4 zones\n- Rider counts: zone 0=8, zone 1=4, zone 2=2, zone 3=1 (total 15)\n- Each driver can carry max 4 riders per wave\n- Total driver capacity: 16 > 15 riders, so we could potentially serve everyone\n\nI'm not fully certain about the price formula \"10 + 5 x (riders - total driver capacity)\" \u2014 its exact interpretation affects strategy. But my hypothesis: if more riders than drivers in a zone, price rises; riders only ride if price \u2264 their private limit (10-60).\n\nSo my initial approach: spread riders across zones to avoid oversaturating any one zone's price. \n\nProposal: \n- 2 drivers \u2192 zone 0 (8 riders: 4 each)\n- 1 driver \u2192 zone 1 (4 riders: all 4)\n- 1 driver \u2192 zone 2 (2 riders: all 2)\n- zone 3 \u2192 1 rider, driver offline\n\nBut I need the team's input on this. What's your read on the pricing, and do you have any thoughts on how to allocate riders?"
      },
      {
        "round": 1,
        "agent": "driver_2",
        "kind": "message",
        "text": "Team planning thoughts for Wave 1.\n\nKey facts:\n- 4 drivers, 4 zones\n- Rider counts: zone 0=8, zone 1=4, zone 2=2, zone 3=1 (total 15)\n- Each driver max 4 riders, total capacity 16 > 15 \u2014 everyone could potentially ride\n\nOn pricing interpretation: I'm reading the formula as price = 10 + 5 * max(0, riders_k - capacity_k), capped at 80. Capacity_k = number of drivers assigned to zone k this wave (\u22644). This means oversaturation drives up price, under-saturation keeps it at base 10.\n\nI propose a distribution that spreads capacity across zones to avoid any oversaturation:\n- 2 drivers \u2192 zone 0 (8 riders: 4 each)\n- 1 driver \u2192 zone 1 (4 riders: all 4)\n- 1 driver \u2192 zone 2 (2 riders: all 2)\n- 1 driver \u2192 zone 3 (1 rider)\n- zone 3 has 1 rider, price stays at base 10, but a driver is offline\n\nThis keeps all zones under capacity (capacity_k \u2264 riders_k for k=0,1,2,3), so all prices would be at base 10. Everyone rides at 10.\n\nBut wait \u2014 the riders have private limits (10-60). If the limit is 10, they're willing to pay 10. If limit is 50, they're willing to pay up to 50. With base price 10, as many as have limit \u226510 will ride.\n\nI'm not sure if base 10 would deter anyone with a limit below 10 \u2014 but the problem says limits are between 10 and 60, so minimum is 10. So base 10 would work for all riders.\n\nActually, I realize there might be an even better approach: we could reduce some capacity to increase base price but wait, base price is already 10.\n\nLet me think about this differently. Maybe we want to concentrate drivers to create some zones with higher capacity than riders, making price stay at 10 for those zones but also... hmm, this doesn't help much.\n\nActually, I think the best approach is simply to spread drivers to avoid oversaturation, so all prices stay at 10 and everyone with limit \u226510 rides. Since minimum limit is 10, everyone rides.\n\nBut I'm uncertain \u2014 what if my price interpretation is wrong? Let me share my assumptions and ask the team for input on the formula reading.\n\nA"
      },
      {
        "round": 1,
        "agent": "driver_4",
        "kind": "message",
        "text": "I want to clarify my understanding of the price formula before committing to an allocation. The key ambiguity is: does \"total driver capacity there\" mean (a) the maximum capacity per zone (i.e., 4), or (b) the actual number of drivers I've assigned to that zone?\n\nMy current hypothesis is (a) \u2014 i.e., base price = $10 for any zone, plus $5 per over-capacity (riders exceed by 1 driver = 4 riders). So:\n- Zone 0: 8 riders, 2 drivers \u2192 price = 10 + 5\u00d7(8\u22124) = $30\n- Zone 1: 4 riders, 1 driver \u2192 price = 10 + 5\u00d7(4\u22124) = $10\n- Zone 2: 2 riders, 1 driver \u2192 price = 10 + 5\u00d7(2\u22124) = $10\n- Zone 3: 1 rider, 1 driver \u2192 price = 10 + 5\u00d7(1\u22124) = $10\n\nBut if the formula is (b) \u2014 actual assigned capacity:\n- All zones stay at base $10 because we can assign capacity to match riders.\n\nI'm not confident enough to commit. Can you share which interpretation you think is correct?"
      }
    ]
  }
